<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:mi="clr-namespace:Material.Icons.Avalonia;assembly=Material.Icons.Avalonia">

    <!-- Where an icon comes from (ET-74).

         Every symbol in this interface used to be a Unicode character typed into a Text or Content: ⚠ ✓ ⧉ ⇄ ☁ 🗑.
         What those look like is decided by the shipped font, not by us, and that went wrong twice in ET-73 — ⬒
         ("square with top half black") drew with its BOTTOM half filled and read on screen as a floppy disk, and the
         thumbtack the operator asked for does not exist as a usable character at all. The answer that round was three
         hand-drawn StreamGeometry shapes, which only moved the problem: a private drawing per window is how a set
         drifts apart.

         So: Material Design Icons through Material.Icons.Avalonia (MIT, ~7500 icons). One package, one control, one
         name per icon — Kind="Pin" is a shape someone else has already drawn at a consistent weight, and it reads the
         same on every machine.

         OUTLINE VARIANTS BY DEFAULT. This app's own icon language is monochrome line art — the module rail (D-182),
         the inbox trash — and Material's solid icons lay down two to three times the ink of the text they sit next
         to, which is exactly the "not heavier than what it replaces" the ticket asks for. Where Material offers an
         outline (TrashCanOutline, ContentCopy, CloudOutline…) take it; only reach for a solid when the icon has to
         carry a state on its own, the way the chips' status dot does.

         SIZE. Three sizes, matching the three text sizes this interface actually uses (9-10px labels, 11px body,
         13px+ headers). Material draws on a 24-unit box with about a unit of margin, so an icon of the same nominal
         size as the text beside it reads a little smaller — hence sizes a touch above what they accompany. A surface
         with its own scale (the module rail, a chip) sets that once for the whole surface rather than per icon; ten
         icons that each size themselves are ten different weights, which is what ET-73 was about.

         COLOUR. An icon takes its Foreground by inheritance, exactly like a TextBlock, so it follows whatever the
         surrounding button or chip has set. Two things follow from that, both learned the hard way here: a state's
         ink has to sit on the ContentPresenter (the stock Fluent theme sets its own there and a rule one level up
         loses to it), and a colour handed to an ATTACHED TextElement.Foreground setter has to be a brush element —
         a "#RRGGBB" string has no target type to convert against and silently resolves to nothing. -->
    <mi:MaterialIconStyles/>

    <Style Selector="mi|MaterialIcon">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="14"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="14"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Beside 9-10px label text: badges, chips, row-level affordances. -->
    <Style Selector="mi|MaterialIcon.icon-sm">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="12"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="12"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Banner and header company: the few places a symbol is the first thing you are meant to see. -->
    <Style Selector="mi|MaterialIcon.icon-lg">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="17"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="17"/>
    </Style>
</Styles>
